          "The 10% demographic gap between the four reported Census categories (92.17%) and 100% is modest but not insignificant in Washington state, where demographic change and voter mobilization have proven capable of shifting competitive races. The gap likely represents mixed-race households, Asian immigrant communities not fully captured by the 4.25% Asian figure, or American Indian/Alaska Native residents—but without specificity, the gap represents unmeasured constituency. More immediately urgent: at 20.3% renters in a high-cost state where renter populations are increasingly mobilized, and with 77.2% of the district being non-college-educated adults, Marshall's 55% margin depends on holding the homeowner, non-degreed working- and business-class base while suppressing renter turnout. If economic conditions produce either renter mobilization around housing costs or defection among working-class homeowners frustrated by stagnant wages relative to rising property taxes and service costs, the 10-point margin is less a floor than a current snapshot. The challenger strategy in 2026 will likely target exactly this economic anxiety among renters and working-class homeowners—making Marshall's defensive priority the affirmative case that current conditions are delivering for ordinary households."
