          { name: "Renter Constituency & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 35% renter rate is substantial enough to represent a significant and politically engaged constituency whose concerns around housing costs and economic mobility diverge from homeowner priorities. In a suburban Washington district, renters — including young professionals, service workers, and families without home equity — are capable of swinging elections when mobilized around cost-of-living issues. A candidate who specifically addresses apartment affordability, tenant protections, and wage growth builds persuadable support that a homeowner-only focus leaves untouched." },
          { name: "Democratic 32% Opposition & Turnout Vulnerability", tag: "careful", why: "The 32% Democratic share in a suburban Washington district reflects meaningful organized opposition. In a state where suburban districts have proven competitive in recent cycles, complacency about a 68% margin is strategically dangerous — the opposition has demonstrated capacity to mobilize, and any decrease in Republican turnout or increase in Democratic turnout relative to 2024 narrows the margin significantly. Understanding specifically what drives the 32% opposition — whether concentrated among renters, younger voters, or educated professionals — and how to either consolidate Republicans against that opposition or directly address swing concerns is essential." },
          { name: "Campaigning as Though 68% Is a Safe Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "In Washington's genuinely competitive suburban environment, a 68-32 margin is comfortable but not commanding. Any candidate who treats this as a firewall and focuses only on consolidating the existing Republican base — neglecting the 35% of renters, the 32% opposition, and the 7.6% demographic gap — risks finding that margin compressed in a cycle where Democratic turnout is higher or a credible challenger emerges. The margin is real, but it requires active defense and constituent engagement, not passive confidence." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 7.6% Demographic Gap & Non-White Constituents", tag: "avoid", why: "With 92.4% of the population accounted for in the four primary Census categories, the 7.6% gap represents a population — likely multiracial residents, immigrants, or other groups — that is real but often invisible in campaign strategy focused only on the white homeowner majority. In a district with suburban diversity increasing over time, a campaign that ignores this 7-8% and the constituencies it represents implicitly concedes that population to opposition mobilization. At 68%, the margin is large enough to absorb some inefficiency, but not large enough to be indifferent to demographic shifts." }
