          { name: "Middle-Class Economic Security & Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $71,692 median household income, WA-HD-6 is solidly middle-class but not affluent—a constituency for whom housing costs, healthcare premiums, and property tax burden represent meaningful financial constraints rather than abstract policy concerns. The 62-38 margin reflects Republican alignment among this homeowner-dominant group, but durability depends on whether the incumbent demonstrates concrete responsiveness to the material economic pressures families actually face. A legislative record that speaks to housing affordability, healthcare cost containment, and tax fairness commands more durable support than one that assumes partisan alignment is sufficient." },
          { name: "Hispanic/Latino Community Economic Integration & Service Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 6.87% Hispanic/Latino population represents a substantial absolute number of constituent families in a typical Washington House district, and their primary economic concerns—wage access, housing affordability, healthcare access—overlap heavily with the white middle-class base rather than representing fundamentally divergent interests. A candidate who builds genuine constituent-service capacity for Spanish-language community members and addresses their specific economic concerns is expanding the coalition beyond the Anglo homeowner base; one who treats Hispanic constituents as administratively invisible or assumes they are locked into opposition is governing a smaller constituency than actually exists and leaving electoral opportunity unattended." },
          { name: "Renter Constituency & Housing Cost Burden", tag: "careful", why: "At 36.3%, renters constitute more than one-third of the district and face materially different economic concerns than the homeowner majority—housing cost burden, lack of asset accumulation, vulnerability to displacement, and accountability to landlords rather than self-governance through property ownership. In a district at $71,692 income, many renters are working adults with genuine economic constraints on housing access, and a legislative agenda that focuses exclusively on homeowner property-tax fairness or mortgage-interest deductions while neglecting renter cost-of-living concerns risks the perception that government is serving the asset-holding majority while neglecting the renters who pay the most as a percentage of income. The 62-38 margin suggests this tension has not yet become electorally salient, but it represents a latent weakness if an opposition candidate mobilizes around renter economic security." },
          { name: "Treating 62% as Structurally Safe", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62% margin in a middle-class district with modest college attainment (27.9%), significant renter population (36.3%), and growing demographic diversity is not a structural floor—it is a margin held by alignment between the incumbent's profile and the current partisan sorting. If a credible challenger emerges who mobilizes the opposition base around cost-of-living issues, healthcare access, or housing affordability, or if the incumbent becomes inattentive to constituent service across demographic groups, the margin can erode more quickly than the current result suggests is possible. Governance posture should reflect the reality that this is a held seat requiring active tending, not a safe seat." },
          { name: "Demographic Blind Spot in the 7.5% Non-White Population", tag: "avoid", why: "The combination of 80.69% white population and 62% Republican result can create an optical illusion that the district is demographically uniform and that representative responsiveness should focus exclusively on the Anglo homeowner base. In fact, 7.5% non-white population—6.87% Hispanic/Latino plus Asian and Black communities—represents real families with real constituent needs (language access, wage concerns, healthcare navigation, discrimination experience). A representative whose constituent-service infrastructure, legislative priorities, and public messaging treat minority communities as peripheral rather than core constituencies is governing less than the full district and creating an opening for a challenger who actively cultivates those relationships." }
