          { name: "College-Educated Voter Engagement & Education Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 37.9% college attainment — above Washington state average — WA-HD-17 contains a significant population of degree-holders whose household decisions are shaped by education quality, university accessibility, and K-12 performance. The 50-50 partisan result in a college-educated suburb suggests that education policy and education funding are swing issues rather than inherited partisan loyalty. A candidate who leads on K-12 funding adequacy, higher education affordability, and the workforce pipeline from education to local opportunity directly engages the decision-making calculus of the district's most educated voters." },
          { name: "Rental Population & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At 27.5% renters in a $102,882-income district, WA-HD-17 contains a meaningful constituency of non-homeowning adults — likely younger households, service-sector workers, and transit-dependent residents whose relationship to the district's affluence is fundamentally different from the homeowner majority. Housing affordability, rental regulation, and transit investment are material concerns for this population, and in a 50-50 race, their turnout and candidate preference directly determine the outcome. A candidate who addresses renter concerns as a legitimate part of district governance, rather than as secondary to homeowner priorities, converts this constituency from latent opposition into active support." },
          { name: "Treating a 50-50 Result as a Stable Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "The 50-50 outcome in 2024 is not a comfortable margin or a demographic floor — it is a deadlocked race in which any candidate who consolidates only the existing coalition and neglects the opposition's drivers will find the seat flipped in the next cycle. A legislature elected with exactly 50% support has no margin for error in constituent service or messaging; any visible failure in addressing the district's actual economic concerns creates an opening for a challenger." },
          { name: "Assuming Partisan Loyalty Among College-Educated Suburban Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated suburban voters in affluent districts are not reliably partisan — they evaluate on competence, fiscal responsibility, and whether government delivers tangible results for their household financial security. A candidate who assumes that the 50% who voted Democratic (or Republican) will automatically turn out again because of partisan identity, rather than because of concrete policy achievements, misreads the decision-making of a constituency that chose a tie in a competitive cycle." }
