          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Property Tax Burden", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 75% homeownership, $102,737 median income, and a 39.4-year median age, WA-HD-18 is a district of established homeowners for whom property tax burden and property value stability are material economic concerns. In Washington's tax environment, this is the primary economic pressure point for affluent suburban households — not income tax (which Washington lacks), but rising property assessments and service costs. A candidate who leads with concrete property tax fairness and school funding mechanisms directly addresses the cost-of-living anxiety that even high-income households experience, and demonstrates governing competence on the issue that most affects constituent household budgets." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Cost Transparency", tag: "lean-into", why: "College-educated voters at $102,737 income have direct engagement with healthcare system complexity and cost volatility — this is not an abstract policy concern but a household budget reality. At 32.4% college attainment, this district has professionals and managers who understand healthcare economics and are evaluating candidates on whether they offer credible solutions to rising premiums, deductible structures, and insurance coverage gaps. Generic Republican healthcare rhetoric will not persuade the 44% Democratic voters in this district; specific answers about insurance market transparency, cost containment, and access equity are required to hold or expand the Republican vote." },
          { name: "Suburban Development & Local Governance Competence", tag: "careful", why: "With 75% homeownership and a college-educated majority, this is a constituency deeply invested in neighborhood stability and local service quality — zoning decisions, development patterns, school performance, and transportation management directly affect property values and family quality of life. The 44% Democratic opposition may reflect frustration with local growth management, school funding, or governance responsiveness, not just statewide partisan positioning. Any candidate must demonstrate genuine competence on the local issues that matter to suburban homeowners, and avoid the appearance of deferring these decisions to distant political ideology rather than constituent needs." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 margin in an affluent, college-educated district where Democratic opposition is embedded in the demographic itself — not geographically isolated or economically alienated — is not a safe floor but a conditional majority. These voters evaluate on governance competence and cost-of-living deliverables, not party loyalty, and any shift in persuasion on healthcare, property tax, or school funding will materially narrow the margin. Candidates who consolidate only the existing Republican base and ignore the opposition's driving concerns will find this seat more vulnerable in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "National Partisan Positioning Over District-Specific Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "This district's 44% Democratic share is not driven by cultural conservatism defection but by voters who live in affluence and are making deliberate choices about economic governance and service quality. Leading with national partisan messaging rather than specific solutions to property tax, healthcare access, or school funding will reinforce the perception that the candidate is not listening to district-specific concerns. Suburban voters at this income and education level will reject candidates who appear more interested in national party positioning than in solving their actual neighborhood problems." }
