          { name: "Housing & Economic Opportunity for Younger Professionals", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 39.5% renter rate in a $104,000-income district signals a significant population of younger professionals, graduate students, and early-career workers who face housing cost pressures even within this affluent context. Economic opportunity, housing availability, and the regulatory environment for small business and professional services are material concerns for renters and younger households in the district. A legislative agenda that addresses housing supply, affordability within the market range, and economic mobility for young professionals deepens Democratic engagement with this constituency." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity & Community-Specific Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "The district is 57.35% white, 16.96% Asian, 10.91% Hispanic, and 5.27% Black — no single group exceeds 17% — with a 2.5% gap likely representing AIAN residents. While the affluent, educated profile cuts across demographic lines, Asian American voters are the second-largest group and a significant part of the Democratic coalition. Educational opportunity, professional-sector access, and community-specific concerns require active engagement; neglecting any demographic constituency in this diverse, high-income district risks allowing a challenger to peel off swing voters within specific communities." },
          { name: "Partisan Base-Building in an Already-Safe Democratic District", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67-33 result in an affluent, educated district reflects sufficient base consolidation — further investment in partisan mobilization and turnout for already-convinced Democrats yields diminishing returns. The strategic vulnerability is not base defection but underperformance on the quality-of-life governance issues that matter most to this constituency. Excessive partisan rhetoric or base-focused campaigning risks communicating to swing voters in the 33% that the incumbent is not focused on the practical governance outcomes that affect their daily lives." },
          { name: "Overestimating the Durability of a 67% Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "While 67% is a solid working margin, it is not a supermajority and not a license for complacency. In a district this affluent and educated, voters are capable of supporting a credible challenger if they perceive the incumbent as failing on schools, transit, public safety, or other material services. Any representative who treats the 67% as a permanent base rather than an earned result that must be renewed through visible delivery will discover that this sophisticated, evaluative constituency will punish underperformance by supporting an alternative." }
