        issues: [
          { name: "Renter Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 48.8% renters paired with $83,424 median income and 32.8-year median age, WA-HD-28-Pos2 is a constituency of young adults navigating housing costs that consume a disproportionate share of income in Washington's housing market. Tenant protection, eviction defense, anti-displacement policy, and down-payment assistance are not peripheral issues—they are the direct material concerns of the demographic majority that produced Bronoske's 58% margin. Any incumbent who underinvests in renter-focused service concedes alignment with the constituency that elected them." },
          { name: "Education-to-Opportunity Pipeline & Student Debt", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 32.8% college attainment and 32.8-year median age signal a constituency with heavy educational investment and early-career earning trajectory. Student debt, higher education affordability, and workforce-development partnerships that translate education into stable employment are the conditions under which this cohort achieves economic mobility. Bronoske's ability to deliver on education-focused policy—funding, debt relief, skills training—directly determines whether the 58% margin holds among voters who are in the thick of these decisions." },
          { name: "Cross-Demographic Coalition Maintenance", tag: "careful", why: "The 58% Democratic margin is not concentrated in a single demographic group: it is built on alignment among 52.53% white, 16.52% Hispanic/Latino, 10.66% Black, and 7.83% Asian voters whose shared material concerns (housing, education, economic opportunity) transcend demographic identity. Any incumbent who takes the cross-demographic coalition for granted—or who allows Hispanic/Latino, Black, or Asian constituents to experience inattention—risks fractionalizing the coalition at its margins. Specific constituent service to each community demonstrates that the majority is held by genuine governing relationships, not demographic momentum." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 42% Opposition as Reactionary or Illegitimate", tag: "avoid", why: "The 42% opposition in a 58-42 seat is substantial and concentrated among homeowners, business-community voters, and those skeptical of Democratic governance on public safety and fiscal discipline. Framing these constituents as simply resistant to progress or aligned with illegitimate interests concedes the ability to move them and forfeits the capacity to hold persuadable swing voters who evaluate on both renter concerns and public-safety performance. The opposition's concerns—retail theft, public-space disorder, business climate—are real governance problems requiring substantive response, not dismissal." },
          { name: "Treating 58% as a Safe Majority Insulated from Challenge", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58-42 result in a district with 48.8% renters, 32.8-year median age, and cross-demographic coalition is not a durable supermajority—it is a working majority built on alignment with a specific economic constituency and dependent on sustained service to that constituency. A challenger with credible positioning on both renter concerns and homeowner/business-community safety can move the margin, particularly if the incumbent becomes inattentive to the opposition's demonstrated concerns or if renter-focused policy fails to deliver tangible relief. This is a seat that requires active coalition maintenance, not the confidence of a 16-point margin." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "WA-HD-28-Pos2: A Young, Renting, College-Educated District at 58-42 Democratic—Hold It by Deepening Service to the Renter Majority and Sustaining Education-to-Opportunity Pipeline",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "WA-HD-28-Pos2 is a demographically young, education-forward, and economically mobile district that Bronoske won decisively at 58-42 in 2024—a margin that reflects genuine Democratic advantage but not immunity. The district's defining characteristic is the 48.8% renter rate, the highest in most competitive Washington state sequences, paired with a 32.8-year median age (also lowest) and 32.8% college attainment. This is a constituency of young adults navigating housing costs, education debt, and early-career earning trajectories in a district where median household income sits at $83,424—comfortable but not insulated from housing-cost pressure. The 58% Democratic margin was earned by alignment with this cohort's material concerns: housing affordability, student debt management, and the pathway from education into stable employment. A Democrat holding this seat must sustain that alignment through concrete service on renter protections, higher education affordability, and housing policy—not through inherited partisan loyalty or abstract progressive positioning.",
          "The district's demographic diversity—52.53% white, 16.52% Hispanic/Latino, 10.66% Black, 7.83% Asian, with ~12.5% gap representing Native American, multiracial, and other communities—creates both an electoral foundation and a governing obligation. Young renters in this income bracket are a cross-racial constituency united by shared material concerns: no single demographic group dominates at the 58% level, which means Bronoske's margin is built on genuine cross-demographic coalition. The 42% opposition is concentrated among older homeowners, higher-income households, and those skeptical of Democratic governance—a constituency that will activate aggressively against any incumbent perceived as inattentive to public safety, business climate, or fiscal discipline. Holding this seat at 58% in 2026 requires doubling down on the renter and young-adult service mission while neutralizing the opposition's capacity to activate homeowners and business-community voters on safety and economic-management grounds. A challenger with credible positioning on both fronts—respecting young-adult economic concerns while addressing homeowner security and business viability—is the most dangerous threat to Democratic tenure."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with renter-specific service: the 48.8% renter rate and $83,424 median income mean housing affordability, tenant protections, and anti-displacement policy are constituent staples, not peripheral issues. Document and communicate specific wins on renter advocacy, eviction defense, and housing cost pressure to sustain alignment with the district's demographic majority.",
          "Sustain the education-to-opportunity pipeline: the 32.8% college attainment and 32.8-year median age signal a constituency heavily invested in educational access and early-career advancement. Higher education funding, student debt relief, and workforce-development partnerships are not abstract policy—they are the material path to stability for most voters in this district.",
          "Neutralize the 42% opposition on safety and economic management: homeowners and business-community voters are the core of the opposition coalition; any credible challenger will activate them on public safety, retail theft, and fiscal responsibility. Respond to these concerns with specific, non-dismissive governance—police funding, public-space disorder reduction, and business-district revitalization—rather than ideological pushback.",
          "Engage Hispanic/Latino, Black, and Asian communities with constituent-specific service: at 16.52%, 10.66%, and 7.83% respectively, these communities are substantial parts of the 58% coalition; renter protections, healthcare access, and workforce opportunity are shared concerns, but community-specific outreach demonstrates that the incumbent is governing for all of them, not a generic 'young adult' category."
        ]
