        issues: [
          { name: "Renter & Working-Household Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 37.8% renter rate, more than one-third of the district's households face direct housing cost pressures that affect every other spending decision—childcare, utilities, transportation, healthcare. With $77,056 median household income, these are households where a 10% increase in rent or utility costs creates real financial stress. A representative who delivers concrete improvements on housing affordability, tenant protections, utility regulation, and cost-of-living relief commands the credibility this constituency requires; a representative who does not will find opposition candidacy mobilized around these specific grievances." },
          { name: "Multiethnic Community Engagement & Differentiated Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 55.27% non-white, with 17.4% Hispanic, 12.43% Black, 10.69% Asian, and ~15% other, WA-HD-29 Position 2 is a genuinely multiethnic district where no single group represents an electoral majority—and where the coalition structure depends on addressing distinct community concerns with specificity rather than a unified minority-community message. The Hispanic community's concerns around workplace equity, immigration policy, and economic mobility differ from the Black community's priorities around police accountability, educational equity, and economic opportunity; engagement that treats these as interchangeable constituencies rather than distinct communities will lose credibility with both. Active, visible constituent service across multiple communities—not token acknowledgment—is the foundation for holding this seat against credible opposition." },
          { name: "Unopposed 2024 Result as Electoral Safety Signal", tag: "careful", why: "Mena's unopposed 2024 victory reflects the absence of a viable Republican challenger infrastructure in Washington state legislative politics, not an absence of constituency volatility on kitchen-table issues. A working-class, renter-heavy, multiethnic district at $77,056 income is structurally competitive when a credible challenger emerges with a concrete alternative on housing, cost-of-living, and public safety. The unopposed result should not be mistaken for a stable floor; any incumbent who assumes electoral safety and reduces engagement with the renter majority, Hispanic community, or Black community creates the conditions for a competitive challenge in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Ideologically Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "WA-HD-29 Position 2 is held through concrete delivery on material constituency concerns—housing costs, utility regulation, public safety, school quality—not through ideological alignment or partisan loyalty. Campaigns that frame this seat as safely Democratic based on partisan registration or cultural politics, rather than on whether the incumbent is delivering measurable improvement on working-household economic security, misread the actual basis of electoral support. A constituency evaluating on cost-of-living and housing affordability is vulnerable to opposition candidacy that addresses these concerns credibly, regardless of partisan affiliation." },
          { name: "Neglecting the 37.8% Renter Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "With more than one-third of households renting, housing costs and tenant protections are not marginal constituency concerns—they are central to how most people in WA-HD-29 Position 2 evaluate their representative's performance. An incumbent who prioritizes homeowner concerns, speculative real estate development, or abstract housing-market principles over concrete renter protections and affordability improvements will find the renter majority mobilized against them, particularly if an opposition candidate articulates a clear pro-renter alternative. The 37.8% renter rate is not a demographic footnote; it is the single largest working-household constituency in the district." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "WA-HD-29 Position 2: A Safe Democratic Seat in a Working-Class Urban-Suburban District with Significant Hispanic and Renter Constituencies—Hold Through Bread-and-Butter Delivery, Not Ideology.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "WA-HD-29 Position 2 is a Democratic-held seat in a district with the demographic and economic markers of a competitive working-class urban-suburban constituency—yet Mena's unopposed 2024 victory signals a lack of credible Republican challenger infrastructure, not necessarily a seat permanently locked into Democratic control. The district's $77,056 median household income, 37.8% renter rate, and 18.9% college attainment reflect a constituency that is economically stable but not affluent, with meaningful asset constraints and housing cost pressures. The racial composition—44.73% white, 17.4% Hispanic, 12.43% Black, 10.69% Asian, and a ~15% gap likely representing other communities—describes a genuinely multiethnic district where no single group represents a majority, and where coalition-building across multiple minority communities is the structural requirement for holding or winning this seat. The unopposed result should not be mistaken for electoral safety; it reflects an absence of opposition candidacy, not an absence of constituency volatility on kitchen-table issues like housing affordability, cost of living, and public safety.",
          "The central strategic fact about WA-HD-29 Position 2 is that it is held not through ideological alignment but through concrete delivery on the material concerns of a working-class, renter-heavy, multiethnic constituency. At 37.8% renter rate—one of the highest possible—housing costs and tenant protections are not abstract policy debates but direct household budget issues for more than one-third of the district's residents. The $77,056 income places the district above bare subsistence but well below affluence; households here are evaluating their representative on whether utility costs, childcare expenses, local school quality, and transit access are improving or deteriorating. The Hispanic community at 17.4% and Black community at 12.43% together represent nearly 30% of the district, and their divergent economic and social concerns—workplace equity, immigration policy, police accountability, and economic mobility—require specific, differentiated engagement rather than a unified minority coalition message. Any challenger who can articulate a concrete alternative on these material concerns, combined with effective outreach to the Hispanic and Black communities, has a pathway to competitive viability even in a seat that currently appears safely Democratic."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with renter and working-household economic security: housing affordability, utility cost management, childcare accessibility, and wage standards are the material concerns of a 37.8% renter, $77,056-income constituency—frame all governance around whether the incumbent is delivering measurable improvement on these fronts.",
          "Build specific relationships with Hispanic and Black communities rather than treating them as a unified minority voting bloc: the 17.4% Hispanic population and 12.43% Black population have distinct economic concerns, workplace issues, and policy priorities that require differentiated constituent attention and targeted outreach.",
          "Do not assume the unopposed 2024 result reflects electoral safety: it reflects an absence of Republican challenger candidacy in a district with genuine workable composition where any credible challenger who addresses housing affordability and cost-of-living concerns can achieve competitive viability.",
          "Engage the multiethnic, non-white-majority character of the district with active constituent service: at 55.27% non-white, the district's electoral future depends on coalition maintenance across multiple communities with divergent concerns—visibility, responsiveness, and concrete delivery on differentiated community priorities are non-negotiable."
        ]
