          { name: "Renter-Majority Housing Security & Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 40.3% renters — the highest renter rate in most comparative contexts — WA-HD-30 is a district where housing stability is a direct economic concern for nearly half the constituency. With median household income at $87,440 and a 56% Democratic margin, younger renters and working families are evaluating whether government is addressing their real cost of living. Rent pressure, tenant protections, and the availability of affordable housing near employment centers are not abstract policy positions — they are material constraints on the constituency's economic security that a winning candidate must address with specificity." },
          { name: "Diverse Community Economic Mobility & Healthcare Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "WA-HD-30's demographic profile — only 38.84% white, with significant Hispanic (20.91%), Black (14.29%), and Asian (13.32%) populations — signals a genuinely diverse working and middle-class district where economic opportunity, healthcare access, and educational mobility are distributed unequally. The 56% Democratic result reflects these communities' electoral alignment, but the margin is not a floor — it is contingent on demonstrable delivery on the specific economic and healthcare concerns these communities face. A representative who engages each community's distinct economic positioning and barriers to mobility, rather than treating diversity as a monolithic constituency, builds durable support." },
          { name: "College Attainment Gap & Workforce Development", tag: "careful", why: "At 28.1% college-educated adults, WA-HD-30 has a lower college attainment rate than many Washington districts, despite the $87,440 median income — suggesting a constituency with mixed educational credentials competing in a knowledge-economy labor market. The 37.6 median age and relatively high renter rate indicate a younger, more mobile workforce where workforce development, vocational training, and pathways to higher-wage employment are not only policy issues but direct economic concerns. This gap between the district's income level and its educational profile represents both a potential frustration point (if young adults feel locked out of advancement) and an opportunity (if a candidate addresses skills training and economic mobility directly)." },
          { name: "Treating the 44% Opposition as a Stable Republican Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 Democratic result in a district with 40.3% renters, 28.1% college attainment, and nearly 62% non-white population is not a safe seat — it is a competitive seat in a state where Democratic margins are under constant pressure. The current Democratic incumbent's 56% is a plurality in a constituency with real economic concerns and a population base that has shown responsiveness to economic messaging. Any Democratic candidate who treats 56% as a permanent coalition floor rather than a margin that requires active engagement with renters, working families, and diverse communities on specific economic concerns will find that support eroding." },
          { name: "Partisan Polarization as a Substitute for Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "WA-HD-30's demographic diversity, high renter rate, and mixed educational profile create a constituency with material economic concerns that partisan rhetoric alone cannot address. Renters need housing policy specifics, working families need healthcare access, and diverse communities need economic mobility pathways — not abstract ideological positioning. Any candidate who relies on partisan activation at the expense of concrete constituent service on housing, healthcare, and economic opportunity will find the 44% opposition mobilizable around a challenger who offers tangible answers to people's actual problems." }
