          { name: "Competence, Fiscal Stewardship, and Government Effectiveness", tag: "lean-into", why: "An affluent, educated constituency at 46.3% college attainment and $97,713 income evaluates on whether government is delivering concrete value—not on partisan signaling or abstract ideology. Public safety, service delivery quality, infrastructure maintenance, and fiscal responsibility are the issues on which this constituency judges Democratic governance as competent or failing. A Democratic candidate in WA-HD-32 who defaults to progressive messaging without demonstrating measurable results on government effectiveness, budget discipline, or service quality will find that the 75% margin softens against challengers who can credibly argue ineffectiveness or waste." },
          { name: "Asian and Hispanic Community Engagement and Policy Specificity", tag: "careful", why: "At 14.8% and 10.91% respectively, Asian and Hispanic communities are large enough to constitute meaningful voting blocs with distinct economic and civic concerns—and both have demonstrated the capacity to evaluate candidates on specificity and competence rather than party default. Immigration policy, business regulatory environment, community safety, and educational quality are issues where these communities may hold nuanced positions that don't align neatly with broader Democratic messaging. Treating these communities as monolithic Democratic constituencies rather than engaging their distinct concerns with genuine specificity risks alienating voters who are evaluating on whether the candidate is actually serving their actual concerns." },
          { name: "Assuming the 75% Margin Is a Stable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75% result in an urban, educated, affluent district reflects genuine constituent alignment with Democratic positioning—but it is not an inevitable or stable baseline. The 25% opposition in a district this blue represents swing voters evaluating on performance, competence, and whether specific governance outcomes are working. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though the 75% margin is secure rather than actively consolidating it will find that margin eroded by credible challengers who can articulate specific failures or competence questions that resonate with an educated, discerning electorate." },
          { name: "Progressive Signaling Without Measurable Results", tag: "avoid", why: "An educated, affluent constituency distinguishes between progressive rhetoric and actual governance outcomes—and will evaluate Democratic candidates critically if policies are not producing measurable results on the issues that matter to their material lives. Housing affordability without supply growth, public safety initiatives without crime reduction, or regulatory agendas that increase costs without clear benefits will erode Democratic support among voters sophisticated enough to distinguish real from performative governance. Defending the 75% margin requires demonstrating results, not defending intentions." }
