          { name: "Multi-Ethnic Community Engagement & Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "With no single racial group exceeding 41% of the population and 59% of the district being non-white, this is a genuinely pluralistic district where no community can be taken for granted. Specific policy commitments to each community — on healthcare access, education quality, economic opportunity, and public safety — and service delivery through culturally responsive institutions signal that representation is authentic rather than generic Democratic coalition-building. Inattention to the specific concerns of Asian, Hispanic/Latino, and Black communities is not a minor oversight; it is the neglect of a majority of the district's constituents." },
          { name: "Young Adult & Working-Class Economic Security", tag: "careful", why: "The 36.4 median age is among the lowest in the state, and the combination of $83,858 income with 48.8% renting suggests a constituency of younger professionals and service workers for whom childcare costs, healthcare premiums, and wage growth are the material concerns that drive voting behavior. A 69% Democratic margin does not signal that these concerns are being met — only that the Democratic brand is preferred. If a competitor articulates the economic pressures of this cohort more authentically, the margin could compress, particularly in a primary context where turnout reflects deeper engagement." },
          { name: "Treating 69% as a Durable Coalition Requiring No Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69% margin in a primary or general election context is not a signal that the coalition can be taken for granted — it is a floor that requires continuous active engagement with the diverse communities that comprise it. The absence of a demographic majority, the presence of a near-majority of renters, and the young age profile all suggest a constituency more likely to reward responsiveness and abandon inattentiveness than a stable, inherited partisan base. Any representative who campaigns as though 69% represents a safe seat is at risk of erosion in a primary if another candidate speaks more authentically to specific community needs." },
          { name: "Generic Democratic Positioning Without Community-Specific Policy Commitments", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 59% of constituents are non-white, and where 48.8% are renters navigating an affordability crisis, generic Democratic messaging about 'opportunity' or 'fairness' without concrete, specific policy commitments to housing affordability, language access, healthcare equity, or cultural community needs reads as performative rather than authentic. Communities that experience their lives through the material pressures of renting, wage work, and minority status distinguish between genuine policy commitment and rhetorical inclusion — and they vote accordingly when presented with an alternative who speaks to their real concerns." }
