          "Engage Hispanic and Asian communities with policy specificity rather than generic diversity rhetoric: at 13.4% and 11.3% respectively, these communities represent a quarter of the district and have distinct education, economic mobility, and immigration policy concerns that differ from white professional households—and require specific legislative attention, not assumed support.",
          "Lead on economic access and affordability as the unifying issue across the district's diversity: the tension between $116k income and 40.9% renter rate, between 51.9% college attainment and minority households' distinct pathways to economic security, is the material reality that connects renters, younger adults, and minority communities across partisan lines.",
          "Do not mistake 84% electoral safety for constituent satisfaction: the 16% opposition and the district's demographic diversity mean that specific constituencies feel unheard—housing, education, healthcare, economic mobility—and an incumbent who assumes the margin is durable rather than actively earning it risks finding a credible challenger emerge around one of these unmet demands."
