          { name: "Subconstituency-Specific Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district's 38.94% white, 25.75% Asian, 16.67% Black, and 9.8% Hispanic composition means no demographic group holds a clear majority — and different communities have distinct legislative priorities. Asian voters prioritize education equity and small business support; Black constituents focus on economic mobility, housing equity, and public safety accountability; Hispanic constituents face workforce and healthcare access concerns. A representative who addresses each community's specific concerns with visible legislative attention builds durable political power in a supermajority district where partisan alignment is baseline, not competitive advantage." },
          { name: "Public Safety and Quality-of-Life Expectations", tag: "careful", why: "The 91% Democratic result does not indicate uniform support for progressive policing abolition or defunding frameworks; educated urban voters at this income level typically expect effective public safety alongside reform. The combination of high income, high education, and high housing costs creates expectations for both responsive policing and crime prevention that must be met with specificity rather than ideology. A representative who cannot articulate a public safety platform that satisfies both reform concerns and community safety expectations will find political vulnerability despite Democratic supermajority status." },
          { name: "Generic Democratic Messaging as a Substitute for Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 91% Democratic supermajority district, the assumption that Democratic partisanship substitutes for differentiated constituent service is strategically fatal. The competitive arena is primary elections, special elections, and the specific service expectations of distinct demographic blocs — all of which evaluate on tangible legislative outcomes and visible attention to their particular concerns, not on partisan label. A candidate or incumbent who runs a generic pro-Democratic campaign without addressing the specific economic and governance concerns of renters, Asian voters, Black constituents, and other blocs will find primary opposition and erosion of coalition cohesion." },
          { name: "Assuming Affluence Eliminates Policy Disagreement", tag: "avoid", why: "The $96,922 median income and 51.9% college education in WA-HD-37 Pos 2 create a constituency that is both affluent and analytically engaged — meaning they evaluate on policy specifics and legislative outcomes, not on assumptions about shared values. A representative who assumes that high income and Democratic partisanship eliminate substantive policy disagreement will misread constituent concerns about housing affordability, education quality, public safety, and government effectiveness. This is a constituency that will change representatives if they conclude the incumbent is not delivering specific, measurable legislative results." }
