          { name: "Housing Affordability and Urban Development Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 26.7% renters and $189,259 income, this district contains both high-income homeowners and young professional renters for whom housing costs are the dominant economic concern — and both groups are deeply affected by zoning policy, development approval, rent regulation, and the interaction between housing supply and pricing. The 34.46% Asian population includes both established homeowners and younger professionals for whom housing represents the largest household expense and a primary measure of whether government is effectively managing the urban economy. A representative who can navigate the complex balance between tenant protection, housing supply expansion, and property-owner concerns with genuine policy sophistication demonstrates competence that this educated, economically-engaged constituency demands." },
          { name: "Asian Community Economic and Cultural Integration", tag: "careful", why: "At 34.46%, the Asian population is the district's largest racial group and a significant component of the professional and business-owning class that anchors the $189,259 median household income. While the district is 66% Democratic overall, Asian voters nationally and in Washington state show responsiveness to economic concerns around business regulation, property rights, and public safety that can create pressure if Democratic governance is perceived as indifferent to these issues. The incumbent should maintain visible, substantive engagement with Asian community leadership, Asian-owned business associations, and cultural institutions — not as token outreach but as genuine partnership on economic policy and community concerns where this population has distinct interests within the broader Democratic base." },
          { name: "Partisan Overreach on Cultural or Social Issues Perceived as Hostile to Business Community", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with 72.8% college education and $189,259 median income is economically progressive but not necessarily culturally radical — and it contains a substantial professional and business-owning class whose tolerance for regulatory expansion or social policies perceived as anti-business is limited. Any Democratic incumbent who alienates this constituency through indifference to business-community input, perceived hostility to entrepreneurship, or overreach on social issues that divide the college-educated professional class risks turning out-of-touch on issues where the district's voters expect Democratic competence, not Democratic ideology. The 66% margin is robust, but it is built on alignment with urban professional-class Democratic values, not on ideological purity." },
          { name: "Taking the 66% Margin for Granted and Neglecting Constituent Service Quality", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% result in a high-information, college-educated, economically-engaged district is not an invitation to complacency — it is a mandate to maintain legislative competence, constituent responsiveness, and alignment with voter priorities at the highest level. This constituency votes on substance and holds elected officials accountable for both results and perceived attentiveness; an incumbent who coasts on the current margin and allows constituent service responsiveness to decline, or who becomes perceived as indifferent to the district's actual policy priorities in favor of abstract partisan positioning, will find that 66% erodes more quickly than the raw electoral data might suggest." }
