          { name: "Asian American Community Engagement and Representation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 15.73% Asian American population is substantially above Washington state average and is one of the district's defining demographic features, yet Asian American voters in affluent urban districts are not a unified bloc and their specific policy concerns can become invisible in a constituency dominated by the white professional majority. Asian American voters in this income and education band may have distinct views on education curricula, business regulation, tax policy, and social issues that deserve specific constituent attention rather than assumption of bloc loyalty. An incumbent who builds authentic relationships with Asian American community organizations, small business owners, and professional networks transforms a demographic plurality into an actively engaged constituency and prevents this community from becoming available to any credible alternative candidate." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability Amid Environmental Regulation", tag: "careful", why: "The 50.8% renter rate in a district with $117,397 median income indicates a mixed housing market where substantial numbers of professionals rent rather than own, creating potential tension between environmental and land-use regulation that increases housing costs and the constituency's desire for affordable housing. A knowledge-worker constituency this educated will evaluate governance on whether policy is delivering tangible improvements in housing supply and affordability, not on the abstract desirability of regulation. An incumbent who acknowledges this tension directly and pursues concrete solutions — zoning reform, development cost reduction, multi-family housing expansion — addresses a material concern of renters and younger homebuyers whose support is real but conditional on visible delivery." },
          { name: "Treating 87% as an Unconditional Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "An 87% result reflects demographic alignment and policy agreement, not unconditional loyalty; in a constituency this educated and this affluent, voter expectations for constituent service, policy delivery, and responsive representation are high, and the assumption that the seat is secure absent active engagement courts the erosion of margin that occurs when dominance becomes disconnection. The 13% Republican share, while small, represents specific constituencies — older homeowners, business owners frustrated with regulation, potential swing voters among Asian Americans — whose defection signals that the incumbent is losing touch with some portion of the district's material concerns. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as if 87% is an unconditional floor rather than a hard-won result that requires continuous constituent service risks the gradual margin decline that makes a formerly dominant seat vulnerable to a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 13% Opposition as Ideologically Irredeemable", tag: "avoid", why: "The 13% Republican result in a district this Democratic is concentrated enough to represent specific constituencies with identifiable governance concerns rather than ideological uniformity; older homeowners may oppose certain regulations or tax policies, some Asian American small business owners may have specific grievances around regulation or labor costs, and some affluent professionals may prioritize tax burden over environmental policy in ways that deserve constituent engagement rather than dismissal. An incumbent who frames the opposition as simply conservative or ideological without investigating what specific policies or governance failures generated their 13% cedes the opportunity to reclaim voters whose opposition is rooted in concrete material concerns rather than abstract ideology. Any candidate who wants to hold or expand this seat must treat the 13% as a diagnostic signal about where governance is failing to deliver for some portion of the district, not as an enemy coalition to be defeated." }
