          { name: "Renter Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 31.3% renters in a district with a $106,029 median income, WA-HD-47-Pos2 has an unusually large renter constituency for its affluence level — signaling younger professionals, graduate students, and service-sector workers who have not achieved homeownership despite high area income. Housing costs, rental market regulation, and pathways to ownership are direct material concerns for this segment, and their frustration with housing policy outcomes can be mobilized by either party. Stearns must demonstrate tangible progress on housing supply and affordability to hold this constituency's support." },
          { name: "Asian & Hispanic Constituency Engagement on Economic Pragmatism", tag: "lean-into", why: "Combined, Asian and Hispanic residents represent 31.48% of the district — a substantial and college-educated voting bloc that does not necessarily align with Democratic base priorities. These constituencies in high-income areas tend to evaluate on entrepreneurship, education quality, fiscal responsibility, and cost-of-living outcomes rather than ideological abstraction. Stearns' 56% margin among these groups cannot be assumed permanent; specific legislative work on small business policy, school performance metrics, and cost control is necessary to maintain their support against pragmatic Republican messaging." },
          { name: "Demographic Heterogeneity & Splintering Base", tag: "careful", why: "With no racial or ethnic group commanding a majority — 49.87% white, 18.32% Asian, 13.16% Hispanic, 8.74% Black, and ~9.9% other — this district's Democratic margin depends on coalition maintenance across groups with potentially divergent priorities. White college-educated voters, Asian professionals, Hispanic residents, and renters of all backgrounds are distinct constituencies whose alignment with Democratic messaging cannot be taken for granted. If any single group perceives the incumbent as insufficiently attentive to their specific economic interests, coalition fracture is mathematically possible at this margin." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with $106k income, 30.8% college attainment, and meaningful Asian and Hispanic representation, a 56% Democratic margin is not safe — it reflects alignment among affluent, educated voters whose partisan loyalty is conditional on legislative performance and whose economic priorities can shift if they perceive the incumbent as ineffective or insufficiently focused on cost-of-living concerns. Any campaign that assumes 56% provides a durable floor without active constituent engagement around housing, renter concerns, and pragmatic fiscal governance risks seeing that margin erode rapidly." },
          { name: "Dismissing Opposition as Ideologically Alien Rather Than Economically Rational", tag: "avoid", why: "The 44% Republican opposition in a district this affluent and educated is not a base-mobilization problem but a reflection of genuine policy disagreement among college-educated professionals around tax burden, government size, and fiscal priorities. Framing this opposition as a consequence of cultural division or insufficient diversity messaging, rather than engaging it on substantive economic terms, cedes persuadable voters to the Republican alternative and treats renter economic security and Asian/Hispanic pragmatism as settled rather than active political questions." }
