          "The district's demographic composition — 66.93% white, 17.16% Hispanic, with 3.98% Asian and 3.67% Black, and a 7.26% gap likely representing other communities — reveals a coalition built across racial and ethnic lines around shared working-class economic interests rather than around cultural messaging or symbolic representation. The 63% margin includes white renters, white small-business owners, Hispanic working families in service and construction sectors, and Asian and Black households navigating a high-cost urban housing market together. Any candidate or incumbent who seeks to hold or expand this seat must understand that the coalition is economically coherent but not demographically homogeneous, and that serving it effectively means addressing the distinct economic pressures facing renters, Hispanic workers, small employers, and established households simultaneously. The opposition's 37% — roughly two-fifths of white voters, some portion of business-oriented households, and any constituencies that feel economically left behind — is the source of any challenger's pathway. Understanding specifically whether the opposition is concentrated among higher-income households frustrated by taxes and regulation, or among working-class voters who feel abandoned by Democratic governance on public safety or neighborhood quality, is essential to understanding whether the 63% floor is defensible or at risk of erosion."
