          { name: "Asian American Swing Voters & Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "At 15.54%, Asian Americans are a substantial constituency in WA-SD-5, and in high-income, college-educated districts they are among the most moveable swing voters. Asian American communities prioritize education quality, economic competitiveness, professional pathway access, and cultural inclusion — issues where both Democratic and Republican messaging can resonate depending on implementation details and credibility. In a 52-48 district, a shift of 3-4 percentage points in Asian American voter participation or preference is sufficient to change the outcome. The incumbent must engage this constituency with specificity on education funding quality (not just quantity), support for professional licensing and credential portability, and whether state policy supports or hinders their economic advancement." },
          { name: "Treating 52% as a Stable Majority in a Genuinely Competitive District", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result is a plurality, not a majority, and in a district this affluent and educated, partisan loyalty is weaker than in less-educated, lower-income constituencies. The 48% opposition represents nearly half the district, concentrates among homeowners and affluent professionals with clear fiscal concerns, and has available to them a Republican party offering tax reduction and deregulation messaging with genuine appeal to their material interests. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 52% is a stable floor, or who assumes that the district's demographics guarantee Democratic loyalty, will be vulnerable to a challenger who takes those fiscal concerns seriously." },
          { name: "Ignoring Hispanic Community Economic & Social Needs in Service-Sector Economy", tag: "avoid", why: "The 7.09% Hispanic population in an affluent district is often concentrated in service sectors, skilled trades, and small business — constituencies with distinct economic concerns around wage floors, workplace protections, credential recognition, and small business access to capital that differ from both the affluent homeowner base and the college-educated professional majority. An incumbent who absorbs the Hispanic constituency into a generic minority-community narrative, rather than engaging their specific economic and workplace concerns, risks losing a constituency that might have viable alternatives in local races. Specificity on whether state policy supports their economic mobility is essential." }
