          { name: "Governance Competence & Economic Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 51-49 margin in an affluent suburban district reflects transactional evaluation, not deep partisan loyalty. Affluent homeowners at this income and education level hold elected officials accountable for concrete results: infrastructure quality, school funding, permitting efficiency, and whether government reduces rather than compounds economic friction in their daily lives. Any candidate who can demonstrate measurable governance wins — completed projects, regulatory streamlining, constituent problem-solving — will consolidate the existing Republican coalition and make the margin durable; any candidate who cannot will find the 49% opposition effectively mobilized." },
          { name: "Abortion Access & Women's Healthcare Persuadability", tag: "careful", why: "In affluent suburban districts with 31.9% college attainment, abortion access consistently outpolls tax reduction among women voters aged 35-55, particularly college-educated women. The 49% Democratic opposition in WA-SD-10 may include a significant persuadable subgroup of Republican or independent women whose support is conditional on abortion-access positions. The incumbent's 51% margin suggests these women are either voting Democratic or sitting out; any candidate who wants to hold or win this seat must demonstrate genuine engagement with women's healthcare autonomy as a governance concern, not dismiss it as a cultural wedge." },
          { name: "Treating 51% as a Safe Republican Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 51-49 result is a plurality of a contested constituency, not a durable majority. In a district with $90k+ income, 31.9% college attainment, and an aging (43.0 median age) suburban profile, the 51% is held by a coalition of affluent homeowners whose loyalty is conditional on governance performance and healthcare access positions, not on inherited partisan identity. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 51% represents a comfortable hold — consolidating the base and ignoring the 49% opposition — is arithmetically and strategically wrong; the margin will collapse if the opposition is energized or the incumbent fails to demonstrate concrete governance wins." },
          { name: "Cultural Grievance Positioning in an Educated Affluent District", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated suburban voters at $90k+ income and 43.0 median age have increasingly distanced themselves from Republican cultural messaging around education, science, and institutional trust. Positioning education, climate science, or diversity as threats to their community reads as dismissive of their actual lived concerns — healthcare costs, infrastructure quality, school funding — and drives persuadable voters toward the Democratic candidate. In WA-SD-10, any candidate who relies on cultural grievance positioning instead of governance competence will find the 49% opposition effectively mobilized and the 51% margin vulnerable." }
