          "Address younger family economic concerns specifically: housing costs, school funding adequacy, and childcare access are the material constraints on young families in a $91,743-income district, and they are more electorally volatile than the older homeowner base. Any challenger will lead with these issues, and the incumbent must demonstrate concrete responsiveness rather than assuming the affluent base is immobilized.",
          "Engage the 5.5% demographic gap with authentic constituent service: whether that gap is AIAN, multiracial, immigrant, or another population, constituents in that space merit direct engagement on their economic and social priorities — not token acknowledgment. In a district this affluent, visible commitment to serving all constituents is both a governance obligation and an electoral safeguard against a credible challenger who might organize the opposition coalition more effectively.",
          "Do not treat 56% as a durable majority: in a highly educated district, the 44% opposition likely includes professionals and young families whose policy concerns are specific and whose voting behavior is less locked into inherited partisan identity than older homeowners. Any incumbent who consolidates only the existing Republican base and ignores what is driving the 44% is vulnerable to a challenger who addresses those concerns directly."
