          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency & Competent Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 43.1% college-educated, WA-SD-22 is a district of professionals and knowledge workers who evaluate elected officials on competence, results, and responsiveness — not partisan loyalty. This constituency brought a 71-29 margin by concluding that Democratic governance delivers better outcomes on schools, healthcare, housing costs, and economic opportunity. Maintaining this advantage requires a legislator who demonstrates problem-solving capability on material issues, communicates with clarity and evidence, and treats constituent concerns as governance priorities rather than ideological tests." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter-to-Homeowner Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 40.9% renters and a $90,071 median household income, WA-SD-22 contains substantial numbers of young professionals in active life transition — building careers, saving for down payments, evaluating whether the region's housing costs are compatible with their economic futures. Housing affordability, rental regulation, down-payment assistance programs, and the feasibility of homeownership for middle-class households are the material governance issues that this cohort uses to evaluate their representative. A legislator who delivers concrete results on housing policy, not just rhetoric, sustains the Democratic advantage among this swing-capable constituency." },
          { name: "Bipartisan Problem-Solving & the 29% Republican Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "The 29% Republican constituency in WA-SD-22 is outnumbered but significant and civic-minded — they vote and participate in district governance. A legislator who dismisses or antagonizes this minority risks consolidating them around a unified alternative and potentially eroding the 71% through perception of partisan divisiveness rather than constituent service. The most durable strategy involves identifying specific issues (school infrastructure, transportation, local economic development) where bipartisan cooperation is possible and building a reputation for listening to all constituents, not just the Democratic majority." },
          { name: "Treating 71% as a Structural Mandate Rather Than a Constituency to Serve", tag: "avoid", why: "A 71-29 result is substantial but not immune to erosion if the legislator assumes it is structural rather than earned through constituent service. College-educated professionals and pragmatic renters are particularly susceptible to switching allegiances if they conclude their representative has stopped delivering on material issues or has become more ideologically performative than results-oriented. Complacency about the 71% creates opening for a credible challenger to consolidate the Republican base and peel off disaffected Democratic voters who felt neglected or ideologically alienated." },
          { name: "Ideological Positioning Over Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "WA-SD-22's college-educated professional base and renter majority voted Democratic because they concluded Democratic governance delivers on schools, healthcare, housing, and economic opportunity — not because they are ideologically committed to progressive positioning on issues distant from their daily lives. A legislator who prioritizes ideological messaging, partisan conflict, or culture-war issues over responsiveness to constituent concerns on schools, transportation, housing costs, and job creation will find this pragmatic constituency becomes receptive to Republican messaging that emphasizes competence and fiscal responsibility." }
