          "WA-SD-25 is a high-income suburban district where the Republican margin of 58-42 reflects a consolidated affluent homeowner majority rather than an overwhelming partisan realignment. The $102,893 median household income and 65.46% white population are the district's dominant demographic signals, and they map almost perfectly onto the Republican coalition: homeowners with strong asset holdings, college-educated professionals (28% college attainment) concentrated in higher-earning occupations, and a 36.3-year median age that skews slightly older than state averages. The 58% result suggests Gildon has successfully anchored this constituency on economic messaging — tax policy, property values, business-friendly governance — that resonates with a district where household income substantially exceeds state and national medians. However, the 42% Democratic floor in a district this affluent is the second critical analytical fact: in heavily Republican Washington state, a 42% Democratic result in a high-income suburban district is not negligible opposition. It reflects either a genuine ideological divide among affluent professionals, or it reflects the emerging demographic composition that the 34.2% renter rate and growing Hispanic (11.82%) and Asian (6.13%) populations signal.",
