          { name: "Hispanic Community Coalition Building & Retention", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 16.5% of the district, the Hispanic population is the single largest non-white community and is mathematically sufficient to shift a 54-46 seat if its support consolidates or fragments. This community's turnout, party preference, and issue priorities determine seat safety; Latino voters in Pacific Northwest urban districts have shown capacity to split their ticket or shift between elections based on economic messaging, immigration enforcement perception, and localized constituent service. Nobles' 54% assumes consolidated Hispanic support; any candidate must maintain active engagement around wage policy, small business development, family economic security, and language access rather than treating this community as a locked Democratic base." },
          { name: "College-Educated Voter Retention in Republican-Trending State", tag: "careful", why: "College-educated voters nationally have shifted toward Democrats even as the overall electorate has trended Republican, and the 32.8% college attainment in WA-SD-28 is a structural asset for any Democratic incumbent. However, this advantage is not permanent: college-educated voters' concerns around economic growth, cost of living, and tax burden can be mobilized by Republican messaging about government efficiency and growth, particularly if inflation or local tax increases create real household pressure. Nobles must actively demonstrate that Democratic policy is delivering for educated professionals — not assume their support is based on social issues alone." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Durable Democratic Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54-46 margin in a state trending Republican is a floor, not a ceiling — it reflects a coalition of college-educated, younger, renting, and non-white voters that must be actively consolidated or it fragments. The 46% opposition includes mobilized Republican voters and persuadable white professionals and parents concerned about education, property taxes, or crime; any assumption that Nobles' 54% is stable without continuous constituent engagement and local problem-solving invites erosion in the next cycle. This is a held seat, not a won seat, and it requires sustained attention rather than management of a comfortable majority." },
          { name: "Neglecting the White Professional Segment of the 52.5% White Population", tag: "avoid", why: "The 52.5% white population includes both college-educated young professionals (likely Democratic-leaning renters) and older, more conservative homeowners (likely Republican-leaning) — treating this as a monolithic voting bloc in messaging will lose the persuadable younger segment to Republicans without defending against the conservative older segment. A candidate who defaults to messaging that alienates all white voters or that tries to win back only the older conservative base will either shrink the coalition or create the opening for a Republican to mobilize the college-educated young professional vote. Segmentation by age and housing status is essential." }
