          { name: "Treating Unopposed Incumbency as Institutional Permission to Reduce Presence", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed victory should not be interpreted as permission to reduce district engagement or constituency service investment. Democratic districts with high diversity, high education, and high income are precisely the districts most likely to attract credible challengers in the next cycle if the incumbent becomes disconnected from constituent concerns. Unopposed status reflects Democratic strength, not incumbent invulnerability; reduced presence would be a strategic error that creates opening for opposition." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Plurality Produces Automatic Coalition Cohesion", tag: "avoid", why: "The assumption that a district's Democratic registration and demographic diversity automatically produce coalition unity is a common error in high-income, high-education urban districts. Asian, Black, and Hispanic communities in WA-SD-37 have distinct positions on public safety, immigration, economic development, and community investment that do not align automatically with each other or with white progressive voters. An incumbent who campaigns as though these communities share identical priorities will fail to address the specific concerns that mobilize each community and will create vulnerability to opposition that does offer differentiated engagement." }
