          { name: "Schools, Transit, & Practical Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "The college-educated (26.7%), professional, 38.3-year-old constituency with $88,701 income evaluates governance on concrete outcomes: school quality, transit investment, commute reliability, and public safety. These voters are not ideologically rigid; they are results-oriented and will reward either party that demonstrates competence on the infrastructure and services enabling families to afford to live and work in an expensive metropolitan area. Specific plans addressing traffic congestion, transit expansion, school funding, and public safety will be more persuasive than abstract positioning on values or ideology." },
          { name: "American Indian/Alaska Native & Hispanic Community Partnership", tag: "careful", why: "The ~10% AIAN gap in the demographic data and 17.25% Hispanic/Latino population together represent roughly 27% of the district whose economic mobility, healthcare access, and civic engagement concerns can become invisible in a district where the dominant story is affluence. These communities have distinct relationships to local government, housing access, and economic opportunity; engaging them as specific constituencies with particular needs—rather than absorbing them into a general diversity narrative—is both a governance obligation and an electoral opportunity in a persuasion-dependent district." },
          { name: "Treating 58% as an Insurmountable Incumbent Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58% Democratic result in a results-oriented, professionally-minded, mixed-tenure district is a comfortable margin but not a base-and-turn-out lock. At 41.3% renter rate and $88,701 income, this constituency will shift its support if current governance fails to deliver on housing affordability, schools, transit, or public safety. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 58% reflects ideological loyalty rather than performance-based evaluation will be vulnerable to a credible challenger offering concrete remedies on the material issues that drive voters' assessment." },
          { name: "Relying on Affluence as a Proxy for Conservative Electoral Appeal", tag: "avoid", why: "The $88,701 median income might suggest a naturally Republican-leaning constituency, but the 58% Democratic result reflects that professional affluence in a high-cost metropolitan area does not correlate with opposition to government investment in housing, transit, schools, or services. This district's voters are affluent enough to afford what they want and pragmatic enough to pay for it; a candidate who assumes affluence equals conservative disposition, rather than engaging voters' material interests on their actual terms, will misread the district fundamentally." }
