          "Address the college-educated, professional 25.7% specifically on workforce pipeline and business environment: higher education funding, workforce development partnerships with local employers, regulatory environment for small business, and tax policy that affects professional households are the policy issues that drive voting among educated affluent voters — not social conservatism alone.",
          "Monitor and specifically engage the 40% opposition: in a high-income, educated district, this vote reflects policy disagreement rather than demographic inevitability. Identify whether opposition is concentrated among younger professionals (rentals, urban-friendly priorities), specific business communities, or educated voters frustrated with state governance competence — and offer concrete remedies rather than assuming the margin is secure.",
          "Recognize the 23% non-white population as an emerging demographic presence, not a margin: Hispanic, Asian, and other communities in a prosperous suburb are economically integrated, not politically isolated. Their concerns around education quality, small-business opportunity, and neighborhood investment overlap with white homeowner priorities — engage them on those shared interests rather than treating them as a separate constituency."
