          { name: "Tax and Spending Trade-offs", tag: "careful", why: "A $189,259 median income district pays substantial state and local taxes; high earners are sensitive to marginal rate increases. The 69-31 margin is decisive but not overwhelming, indicating 31% Republican support among affluent voters. Messaging must clearly articulate ROI on public investment rather than assume unlimited appetite for tax increases, even among Democratic voters." },
          { name: "Immigration Restrictionism", tag: "avoid", why: "Asian residents comprise 34.46% of the district and Hispanic/Latino residents 6%; together they represent 40% of the population. Anti-immigration rhetoric activates opposition among a significant, affluent, and politically engaged bloc. This messaging risks eroding the 69% margin by consolidating non-white voters against restrictive candidates." },
          { name: "Austerity and Public Service Cuts", tag: "avoid", why: "The high college-education rate (72.8%) correlates strongly with preference for robust public institutions and infrastructure investment. The district's median age of 41.3 and high income suggest voters have already benefited from quality schools and want to maintain them. Austerity framing contradicts the district's demonstrated values and invites Democratic consolidation against such messaging." }
