          { name: "Service Delivery & Government Efficiency for High-Income Professionals", tag: "lean-into", why: "A district with 72.7% college attainment and $156,826 income evaluates government on concrete outputs: quality schools, functional transportation, reliable utilities, public safety, and cost-efficiency. High-income professionals have alternatives—they can relocate to other regions, enroll in private schools, or hire private services—and they will do so if they perceive government as failing to deliver. The Democratic incumbent's durability depends on demonstrating that state and local government is delivering measurable quality-of-life improvements; a challenger who positions incumbent governance as inefficient or failing to solve concrete problems (traffic, housing, school capacity) has a credible opening into a constituency with both the resources and the willingness to exit." },
          { name: "Asian-Plurality Community Engagement on Policy-Specific Issues", tag: "careful", why: "At 38.47%, Asian residents represent the district's largest minority community and, in recent electoral cycles across multiple states, have demonstrated capacity to shift substantially based on specific policy performance rather than fixed partisan identity. Education policy, immigration reform affecting skilled-worker immigration, and business regulation are the issues this community evaluates most directly. Democratic incumbents cannot assume this constituency is structurally Democratic; Asian voters in high-income professional districts are responsive to concrete policy outcomes on education quality, school choice, and whether immigration policy serves their economic and professional interests. Authentic engagement on these specific policy domains, rather than generic multicultural messaging, is required to maintain this community's support." },
          { name: "Treating 71% as Structural Democratic Supermajority Rather Than Current Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "A district where 58.6% of residents rent, the median age is 35.9, and high-income professionals dominate is precisely the constituency most likely to shift if economic conditions deteriorate or government performance disappoints. The 71-29 margin reflects current alignment, not durable Democratic structural advantage. Any incumbent who campaigns on the assumption that this margin is stable and requires only base mobilization will be blindsided by a credible challenger who understands that high-income renters evaluate on tangible outcomes and have the resources to relocate if dissatisfied." },
          { name: "Generic Multicultural Outreach Without Policy Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "High-income professionals of any ethnicity—Asian, white, Hispanic—evaluate government on concrete policy delivery, not on symbolic representation or generic diversity messaging. In a district with 38.47% Asian residents and 72.7% college attainment, candidates who offer vague commitments to 'community investment' or 'equity' without specific policy proposals addressing housing, education, or tax efficiency will be perceived as inauthentic. The swing voters in WA-SD-48 are sophisticated consumers of political messaging; they respond to credible policy analysis, not rhetorical gestures." }
